#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "039f9f0b-70dd-5360-9240-2260ff706c93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Coefficient in a Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85daf819-c485-5b76-9b07-bac1a22b4c4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292768} {:answer/id #uuid "9f57052c-5479-5e54-bf26-0da42f1ae4ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292769} {:answer/id #uuid "b10e3352-8230-5606-9b92-938172d27c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292770} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe63a00-9d88-54e1-a08f-f0e2fcaa50c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292771} {:answer/id #uuid "0e7d414f-7314-5ebc-9fe3-07d4c12d3519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85daf819-c485-5b76-9b07-bac1a22b4c4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292768} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac18cc15-e783-5eb8-8c37-9be083dc6b70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0165882d-d9e6-5904-8c7d-cc258a668fd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-125129" :question/problem "What is the coefficient of $(x-1)^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-{x}^{3}}$ at $x=1?$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-1)^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(1).\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-2x{e}^{-{x}^{3}}+3{x}^{4}{e}^{-{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1) & =-2\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{-{1}^{3}}+3\\cdot {1}^{4}\\cdot {e}^{-{1}^{3}} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-1}+3{e}^{-1} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x-1)^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2} & =\\frac{{e}^{-1}}{2}=\\frac{1}{2e}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
